4.1 Label variables in plain words
A figure or table is read by people, not by the code that made it. Replace every variable name from the data with a label a reader understands:

turn underscores and other code punctuation into spaces;
use ordinary capitalization;
spell out abbreviations and acronyms where space allows;
give the unit of every measured quantity, in parentheses;
relabel category levels the same way, including legend entries.

Use the same label for a variable in every figure, table and paragraph, so a reader can match them up.







Example 1 (Relabeling axes and a legend)  

❌ x-axis age_yrs, y-axis sbp, legend trt_grp: ctrl, tx
✅ x-axis “Age (years)”, y-axis “Systolic blood pressure (mm Hg)”, legend “Treatment group”: “Control”, “Treatment”

In R with ggplot2, set the axis and legend titles with labs(), and recode the category levels before plotting, for example with forcats::fct_recode().

1.8 Give every relationship stated in words a formal expression
When a sentence states a mathematical relationship, also write that relationship as a formal expression. A relationship is any claim that links quantities, such as:

an equality or an inequality;
a limit;
a proportionality;
a bound or an ordering;
a “depends on”.

Keep the sentence, because it tells the reader what the relationship means, and add the displayed formula, because it tells the reader exactly what the relationship is. Prose alone leaves the reader to guess the quantities, the direction, and the conditions. Put every condition of the prose in the formula too, and introduce its symbols in the sentence. Keep the sentence and the formula separate, as the rule to write each sentence in words or in math requires: the display may follow a lead-in such as “In symbols:”, and the formula is not part of the sentence in words.







Example 5 (Adding the formula to a prose relationship)  

❌ If random variables \(X\) and \(Y\) are independent, the variance of their sum is the sum of their variances.
✅ If random variables \(X\) and \(Y\) are independent, the variance of their sum is the sum of their variances. In symbols:
\[X \ind Y \implies \Var{X + Y} = \Var{X} + \Var{Y}.\]

The ❌ version leaves the reader to reconstruct the formula and its condition. The ✅ version keeps the sentence and adds the relationship in math, with the independence condition inside the formula.

4.2 Methods
Give enough detail that another analyst could reproduce the analysis:

study design and setting, with the dates of each period;
data sources and how records were linked;
participants: eligibility, exclusions with counts, and a flow diagram when the exclusions are not trivial;
the exposure or intervention, including its timing;
primary and secondary outcomes, with units and how each was measured;
covariates, and why they were included;
the statistical analysis:

the estimand;
the model, its assumptions, and how they were checked;
handling of missing data;
clustering or other correlation;
multiple comparisons;
sensitivity analyses; and
software, with versions;

ethics approval or exemption, and consent or its waiver; and
the reporting guideline that the paper follows.

4.4 Discussion
A standard Discussion runs through:

the principal findings, in one paragraph, without repeating the numbers;
comparison with prior work, with citations;
interpretation and possible mechanisms, labeled as interpretation;
limitations, each with its likely direction and what was done about it, covering whichever of these apply:

confounding;
selection;
measurement;
generalizability; and
power; and

implications for practice, policy, or research.
